// A parameter's borrow copied into a local, then reborrowed through that
// local and returned: the reborrow points at what the parameter points at,
// the caller's, so it may be returned. The local holding the borrow is not
// what the reborrow points at.
//
// Verdict: accept; prints 8.
// Basis: reading of the language rule (1 Oct 2026) that a variable holding a
// borrow has no lifetime of its own; the lifetime is part of the borrow's
// type -- 'q''s type carries the caller's lifetime, and '&mut *q' borrows its
// target. refborref.html, "Lifetime
// Constraints", says a borrow "from ... another reference" is bounded by the
// block where it is taken; that sentence is stricter than the rule and the
// stage that lands this rewrites it ("the lifetime of what that reference
// points at"). Rust: accepted -- 'let q = p; &mut *q' returned from
// 'fn f(p: &mut i64) -> &mut i64' (by reading, not run).
// Master d331baab: refused, ErrorEscape at '&mut *q' -- safe code refused.
// Stage 1 fixed it.

mod lifetime_band_return_reborrow_through_local;

import stdio use *;

fn show(label &str, n i64) {
  printStr(label);
  printStr(" = ");
  printInt(n);
  printStr("\n");
}

fn f(p &mut i64) &mut i64 {
  imm q = p;
  &mut *q;
}

fn main() {
  mut k = 7i64;
  imm r = f(&mut k);
  *r = *r + 1;
  show("reborrow-through-local", *r);
}
